{# narrative_engine/plot_adapt — what a schemer does after a step fails.
   Asked once per failure by PlotPlanning::Adapt, until the plot succeeds,
   is given up, or spends its attempts. The answer is substitute (find
   another way, leaving the failed route behind) or concede. There is no
   retry: a failure means the route it was on does not work.
   Receives: mastermind { name }, ambition, scheme, steps [string] (what
     has happened so far, numbered, with how each went), failed (the step
     that just failed and how), attempt, max_attempts, options [{ key, line }].
   NOTE: no fenced blocks and no literal braces anywhere in this file. #}
[ state aims ]
{{ mastermind.name }} is ultimately after this: {{ ambition }}
The scheme they are working on: {{ scheme }}
[ end state ]

[ state history ]
{% for s in steps %}{{ s }}
{% endfor %}
[ end state ]

[ state setback ]
The step that just failed: {{ failed }}
This is attempt {{ attempt }} of {{ max_attempts }} to recover the scheme.
[ end state ]

[ question next choice ]
Given the scheme in `aims`, what has happened in `history`, and the setback in `setback`, what does `aims`' schemer do now? The way that failed, and everything it was there to make possible, is closed to them. Finding another way suits a scheme that can still be reached by a different road; giving up suits one that has cost more than it is worth or can no longer work.
[ criteria ]
{% for o in options %}{{ o.key }}: {{ o.line }}
{% endfor %}[ end criteria ]
[ end question ]
